# Chapter 1

The whole bloody business began just outside the crumbling walls of St. Kiro's, at a bend in the Nara where a band of Forewalkers kept their scrap nets. St. Kiro's, as the place was named in glittering Belatori glyphs on the maps of the city that the Lord Fathers kept in Le Coron, was a sleepy burg well outside the city proper that was really only notable for being the last stop on the Dawn Line of Cassoloro's train system. It was not, generally speaking, the sort of place where anything of note happened. Thus it would be of some surprise to anyone who knew anything about city affairs that Cosara Le Cun, Lord Father Lyrrico's own hound-man, had stepped out of a carriage at the edge of St. Kiro's one misty spring morning, come to inspect a massacre.

The three of them must have been a strange sight for anyone out in the fields that morning, Cosa thought. Himself, Sabón, and Derra were not the sort of folk anyone around here was used to seeing. Eached of them was dressed in pristine Lyrico livery, yet each looked wholly unsuited for such finery. Cosa's face was much too scarred and pockmarked by old wounds and older diseases to ever really fit in with his clothing, and Derra's frayed, untamed nest of hair was hardly the sort of thing one might expect to top the head of a noblewoman. Only Sabón was kempt enough to really fit the mold, but if one looked close enough at him they would notice the paleness of the skin and the sharpness of teeth, signs of the distant taint in his blood. 

No, none of the three were the sort of people one might expect to be representatives of the most powerful man in the city, indeed in all the isles of Lorcente and perhaps the entire continent, and yet representatives of that man they were. Power had no use for good looks when it comes to matters as sensitive as this. 

A man in the golden-yellow robes of the City Guard walked up to meet the three of them. A brass eagle pinned to his shoulder marked him as a captain.

"You are Lyrico's men, then?"

"We are," Cosa said, shaking the man's hand. As he did, he slipped a few bits of gold to him, for his loyalty. The captain took them without acknowledgement. 

"My men have kept the place clear," said the captain. "If you have further need of me, you can find me at the parish house. Ask for Captain Gucero."

"Many thanks, captain," Sabón said, in his immaculately tailored Cassolori accent. "Our patron will remember your loyalty, **and your discretion.**" The tone of his statement was of thanks and threat both.

The captain nodded, then barked orders to his men. The group dispersed quickly, leaving Cosa, Sabón and Derra alone in a field of dead men.

"Well," said Sabón, his nose held slightly aloft as if the view offended his delicate sensibilities. "This is a right mess, now isn't it? When was the last time you saw this many dead men, Cosa?"

**When our patron came with his airships to put down the revolution,** Cosa thought, but he said nothing. 

"Whoever killed these men had a good arms dealer," Derra said, having knelt down to inspect one of the bodies. Her skirts were already covered in mud. "The bullets were alchemicaly infused to be explosive."

"No ordinary band of thugs, then," Sabón said. "Though I suppose that is to be expected. An ordinary band would know better than to attack a Lyrico caravan, wouldn't they?"

"I wouldn't be so sure," Cosa said. "The world has plenty of fools."

"Fools with access to alchemical weaponry?"

Cosa gave Sabón a disappointed look at that particularly ill-thought-out comment. "Ah, yes," Sabón answered. "I suppose you're right. Time to get to work then, boss?"

Cosa nodded, and they set about it. It was simple work, really. Check the bodies, the wagons, and everything within a dozen strides of the scene. Make sure that when the morgue crew came, they didn't find anything they weren't supposed to. This caravan wasn't **supposed** to be moving anything sensitive, just several crates of gunpowder, a few dozen Kessen rifles, and a few items for private parties. Then again, arms were an inherently sensitive business.

"Oh dear," Sabón said, kneeling down to inspect a corpse. "You'll want to have a look at this, Cosa."

It didn't take long for Cosa to figure out why Sabón was so concerned. Half of the poor fellow's face had been blown off, with one exception. His left eye was unnaturally suspended in the air, nerves and veins blowing in the wind. A closer inspection revealed other oddities. Bones in the shape of bullets growing from the ruins of his skull, impressions of inhuman faces left in the smear of his brain. A subtle bit of rending, as far as those Cosa had seen, but no less horrifying. 

There were many good reasons that, until recently, all the nations of the continent had condemned sorcery as blasphemy. A man might be able to imitate the works of gods, but his imperfect mind always left blemishes behind.

"Eugh," Derra grimaced. 

"Quite, quite, my dear," Sabón said. "No ordinary band of thugs indeed. This is not the work of some hedge-sorcerer. The rending is subtle. These were professionals."

"Then why did they leave the bodies?" Cosa said. "Why leave evidence of their crime?"

"Perhaps as some kind of message," Sabón said. "Or perhaps they were just in a rush."

Cosa grunted. He did not like the looks of any of it. "Search the wagons, and find what's missing. Whoever did this was after something."

Whoever these robbers had been, they were greedy, or at least wanted to appear tso. Most of the rifles had been taken, as had much of the incidental weaponry. Several of the private packages had been taken, too, despite the fact that most had been stored in alchemical lock boxes which were, as a rule, nearly impossible to open without the right key. 

Several of the dead caravan guards showed signs of the same sorcery. Scars in impossible shapes and wounds that sprouted canine teeth. Grisly as it may be, though, this was the work of a skilled sorcerer. Cosa had seen the work of amateurs, and it was much less pretty. The sort that could get a man hanged from the spires of the Lady Grace's Basilica as an example to street sorcerers.
